#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079637 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a right triangle $\\triangle ABC$ with legs $AC= BC = 1$, like the one in the diagram below.\nHow can we find the value $\\sin \\theta$ if we don't know one of the relevant sides?\n\n![](images/ae/ae5157e6824fd98861194302ab912e76d9e79dade6706dfbedea97d859f18a79.png)\n\nRecall the definition of sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta = \\dfrac {\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac {AC}{AB} = \\dfrac 1 c\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $c$. The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc &= \\sqrt{AC^{2} + BC^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can finish computing the value of $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= \\frac{1}{c} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{1 \\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNo matter which trigonometric function we need to find, if we are missing one of the relevant sides but we know the lengths of the other two sides, we can always use the Pythagorean Theorem." :tutorial/id #uuid "75942071-e229-5a18-a349-e9a0df09faba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2446 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
